d not fully aware of  all the data because you can never  really convey all the data or or the  strength of it to  a non-scientific public when it comes to  something like  coal which relates to the climate um  a debate more generally in that  certainly you know if you disbelieve the  science on climate  you might be more supportive of coal  because of its immediate  sort of monetary benefits in that it's  cheap and we don't need  new infrastructure like we would with  solar or wind or whatever the case may  be  how have you seen or sort of observed  the coal debate happened during the  current administration where we've seen  the president  essentially say that he wants to take  take this  out of the market the free market of  course pointing to letting coal die  to revitalize an industry that from a  climate perspective doesn't make sense  where does this fit into the discussion  in terms of people who  who say yeah we should we really should  bring coal back is it a denial  of the climate science is it a sort of  political or tribal based reaction  some combination of the two yeah i think  it's it's certainly a combination of the  two and it's certainly a reflection of  the fact  that um to the extent the coal industry  has been defending itself in the past by  saying that you should let the market  decide  that sort of ideological justification  for burning coal  really wasn't what was ever motivating  them and  in fact what the president has tried to  do is to get uh  power companies to buy coal and and  subsidize coal  even when they would prefer not to so so  that was  a pretty clear uh illustration that  the ideological justification was never  really what was driving it  the last thing i want to touch a touch  on is i i think if we didn't mention  this we would be missing a huge piece of  this which is the denial side is often  very well funded  and when you think back to tobacco and i  forget if what if it was the clean  skies or clean air initiative or  w